6 Report the assumption, not only the name
For each result, state:

whether trends are unconditional, observed-feature conditional, or latent;
which features the estimator receives;
whether the outcome relation is linear or nonlinear;
whether assignment is confounded;
whether errors are serially or cluster dependent;
the ATT target and its true value.

These details make the experiment understandable without private context.

6 Estimator adapters
Keep adapters small and local:
def fit_matrix_estimator(panel, estimator):
    outcome, treatment = panel.as_arrays(copy=True)
    return estimator.fit(outcome=outcome, treatment=treatment)
An adapter may reshape data or rename columns. It should not change the simulated values or recompute known effects. Several estimators can then use the same panel.

5 Read-only results
PanelDataset copies its input arrays and marks them read-only. This prevents an estimator from changing stored untreated outcomes or effects by accident. Metadata and annotations are also frozen.
The result supplies:

as_arrays() for matrix estimators;
as_long_dict() for data-frame and formula estimators;
true_att for the average realized treated-cell effect;
truth.cohort_event() for supported cohort-by-event-time cells;
truth.event_study() for event-time aggregates;
truth.att_by_cohort() for cohort ATT values.

4 Why this is not the first benchmark
A composite design is useful only after simpler cases have been checked. Start with the canonical designs, then add one complication at a time:

two-way fixed effects;
one strong factor;
treated-control loading separation;
time-series persistence;
sparse donor structure;
the full composite.

2 Panel design
The default panel has 1,200 units, 36 periods, and nine adoption cohorts. The untreated outcome combines unit effects, time effects, periodic time terms, and errors correlated across units and time.
Each named variant changes only the cohort-specific effect paths. The variants cover:

complete or common-path homogeneity;
cohort blocks, time blocks, and cohort-time tensor blocks;
early-versus-late cohorts and three time epochs;
smooth dose gradients and response-timing differences;
local shocks and damped waves;
fully cohort-specific signatures, peaks, or signed paths.

3 Factor overlap
In the factor designs, overlap=0 gives treated and control units similar loading distributions. Larger values separate those distributions.


